  "sections": {
    "work": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Caravaggio chose what he thought the moment of greatest dramatic impact: the moment of decapitation itself.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Judith Beheading Holofernes (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Beheading_Holofernes_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The figures are set out in a shallow stage, theatrically lit from the side, isolated against the inky black background.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Judith Beheading Holofernes (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Beheading_Holofernes_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "X-rays have revealed that Caravaggio adjusted the placement of Holofernes' head as he proceeded, separating it slightly from the torso and moving it minutely to the right.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Judith Beheading Holofernes (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Beheading_Holofernes_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "moment": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "Artemisia Gentileschi and others were deeply influenced by this work.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Judith Beheading Holofernes (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Beheading_Holofernes_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The exhibition catalogue (Skira, 2018, p88) also cites biographer artist Giovanni Pietro Baglione's account that the work was commissioned by Genoese banker Ottavio Costa.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Judith Beheading Holofernes (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Beheading_Holofernes_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      }
    ],
    "matters": [
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The model for Judith is probably the Roman courtesan Fillide Melandroni, who posed for several other works by Caravaggio around this year; the scene itself, especially the details of blood and decapitation, were possibly drawn from his observations of the public execution of Beatrice Cenci in 1599.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Judith Beheading Holofernes (Caravaggio)",
          "readAt": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Judith_Beheading_Holofernes_(Caravaggio)",
          "accessed": "2026-11-04",
          "tier": 5,
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0"
        }
      },
      {
        "kind": "quote",
        "text": "The faces of the three characters demonstrate the artist's mastery of emotion, Judith's countenance in particular showing a mix of determination and repulsion.",
        "source": {
          "author": "Wikipedia contributors",
          "title": "Judith Beheading Holofernes (Caravaggio)",
